//! # State machines
//!
//! A machine declares a closed set of events, states, and transition rules.
//! `step` mutates a machine value, and `state_name` returns its current state.
//! Every state/event cell must be covered. `default { state }` is the explicit
//! no-op stay rule for all cells not otherwise listed.
//!
//! ```hew
//! machine Counter {
//!     events { Inc, Reset, }
//!     state Zero,
//!     state NonZero { value: i64 },
//!     on Inc: Zero => NonZero { value: 1 }
//!     on Inc: NonZero => NonZero reenter { value: state.value + 1 }
//!     on Reset: NonZero => Zero,
//!     default { state }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     var counter: Counter = .Zero;
//!     let _ = counter.step(.Inc);
//!     let _ = counter.step(.Inc);
//!     println(counter.state_name());
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! A rule head names the state the transition produces, so the body carries
//! only what the head cannot: nothing for a unit target, the field list for a
//! fielded one, and an expression when the value is computed. `state.field`
//! reads the source state and `event.field` reads the event payload; `self` is
//! an actor or method receiver and is not bound here.
//!
//! `reenter` marks a self-state transition that re-enters the state's
//! lifecycle rather than being treated as an ordinary stay. It is useful when
//! a self-transition must rebuild state or trigger state-entry behaviour.
//!
//! Wildcards are asymmetric: `_` is allowed for a state pattern, but events
//! must be named explicitly. For example, `on Kill: _ => Dead,` covers Kill
//! from every state, but there is no wildcard-event spelling. Explicit cells
//! win over wildcard-state rules. Omitting a cell without a matching default
//! is a compile error, so state and event exhaustiveness is enforced rather
//! than inferred at runtime.
